From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:42:21 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] ui/sdl2-wl-scanout: give a plane its producer's buffer

The plane handoff (q11-066) hands weston each guest plane as the export
of its GL texture. When the texture is a decoder's output -- the video
of a WebView, a layer of its own under the app's window or among the
HMI -- the decoder's SAND buffer is published for it (q11-071), but
only a lone full-screen scanout ever took it, so the decoder converted
every frame into the texture on the GPU and weston was handed that.

With /run/qemu-plane-sand a plane whose texture has a current entry with
a fourcc gets the producer's buffer instead, as the full-screen path
does: the WlScanBuf of that path (asynchronous create, the owner told
hold(true) once the plane shows it and hold(false) on weston's release),
with a viewport whose source is the texture's rectangle in the buffer's
pixels (DirectScanout.src_w/src_h give the texture area a larger buffer
fills; 0 means 1:1) clamped to the buffer, so the HVS scales a 4K frame
onto a 1080p plane. A buffer is not taken when it names another
virtio-gpu resource than the plane's (the GL name went to a new one) or
has another size than the texture without saying what area it fills (the
Raspberry Pi 4's proxy). The flush is a hit when a plane got one and
every plane whose texture has an entry got its; it is reported before
any texture is exported, so a miss makes the skipped conversions first
(sdl2-gl.c reports a false return before its blit). A producer may skip
only while the last flush, a hit, took its buffer
(direct_scanout_note_shown), so a second decoder whose video DomA
composes on its GPU, on no plane, does not skip; and a flush is a hit
only if every producer with conversions to catch up had its buffer
taken. A miss runs the catch-up of every such producer, a bounded number
of conversions each, and a producer stays listed until its catch-up is
done (the registry kept only the last one's before). The decoder's
catch-up makes at most 12 conversions a miss and says when none is left;
the Raspberry Pi 4 proxy's (virtio-media.c) makes all of its copies at
once. The proxy asks to skip as itself too: its buffer is taken in the
full-screen path; in the plane layout the proxy's entry is taken only at
the texture's own size, unless the proxy says what area of the texture
it fills. A buffer the planes attach is no longer the direct path's last
commit, so that path commits it again when it takes over.

A hit lets the producer skip conversions in this layout too (decided
2026-10-19, against keeping the conversions): should DomA compose the
video on its GPU meanwhile -- a screenshot, a client composition -- it
samples what the texture was last converted to, an older frame of the
stream or none yet. Seen on the board with screencap while a 1080p video
played among the HMI: consecutive screenshots went back and forth
between two frames. Without skipping, the same layout cost V3D 18 -> 48%
at 1080p and 4K60 in a WebView fell from 42 to 19 frames shown a second.

A fourcc buffer larger than the window is attached whole by the
full-screen path, and SDL 2.32's own wp_viewport on the window surface
shows it at the window's size; without wp_viewporter it is refused,
since SDL then has no viewport either.

Two cases are left as they are. A buffer the full-screen path attached
stays on the window surface, and its frame held (in DomD's CMA, 12.4 MB
at 4K, also after its decoder closed), while the planes have the
display, until that path commits again; plane 0 is opaque and a
scaled primary has the black backdrop under it, so it is not seen
(without the dma-buf heap there is no backdrop). A wl_buffer weston
still has to release when it is attached again -- also when a plane
that moves is unmapped and mapped again in one present -- would see
that release clear its state; weston releases a buffer once, when no
surface has it any more, and this was not seen to go wrong.

Measured on a 16 GB Raspberry Pi 5 running the 8g image, DomD's CMA
640 MiB, DomA's scanout buffers linear (vendor.minigbm.scanout_linear)
and q11-082 (2026-10-19):
  - HEVC 1080p60 in a WebView among the HMI: 58 frames shown a second,
    V3D render 18.3-18.8%, DomA's output on five planes (the black
    backdrop, the SAND frame on an overlay scaled to 1614x908, three
    AB24 layers), hvs_underrun 0. Without plane-sand the video is the
    texture's UIF export, which no vc4 RGB plane takes (expected, not
    measured in this layout, with the decoder's output not linear then).
  - HEVC 4K60 full screen in the WebView: 41.8-43.2 frames shown a
    second in five runs (25.3 with neither this nor q11-082, and the CMA
    then 384 MiB), V3D render about 22% where it was recorded, on two
    planes (the 3840x2160 SAND frame scaled to 1920x1080 on the primary,
    the app's window as AB24 on an overlay).
  - the same 4K stream among the HMI: 42.3-43.9 frames shown a second
    in three runs, the SAND frame on an overlay scaled to 1614x908.
Not measured on the Raspberry Pi 4 when this was written (the display
toggles of that build left the switch off); its proxy's skipping with
the changed registry was not run there either.

Left for later (2026-10-27, Raspberry Pi 4 with plane-sand and
sand-skip-isp on): the proxy's catch-up runs on the main loop with the
BQL held and makes all of its skipped copies at once, 35-41 ms for 9
frames. With DomA's Wi-Fi relay, its uplink interface and the WebView's
transport fixed outside qemu, steady full-screen playback made no miss;
a pause still makes one, when DomA's drm_hwcomposer flattens the still
scene after 1 s and composes it on its GPU. Accepted while no other
sound plays alongside a paused video (no such use case yet); if one
comes, stop the guest's flattening or convert only the resource on the
display (a comment at virtio_media_isp_catch_up says the same).

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
